From df5b676c1ad58da137a20f37308e816db43dfc19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Miroslaw Dach <mdach@lbl.gov>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 08:39:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] bsps/arm/raspberrypi: Fix IRQ dispatch with bank-1 IRQ
 pending

bsp_interrupt_dispatch() reads BCM2835_IRQ_BASIC and, when any speed-up
bit is set, calls ffs() on the unmasked value.  Bit 8 ("one or more bits
set in pending register 1") is also set whenever a bank-1 interrupt
without a speed-up bit is pending, for example the system timer compare
3 used for the clock tick (GPU IRQ 3).  If such an interrupt is pending
together with a speed-up one, for example USB (GPU IRQ 9, speed-up bit
11), the register reads 0x900.  ffs(0x900) - 1 = 8,
bcm2835_irq_speedup_table[8] is -1, the vector becomes 0xffffffff, no
handler is called, the interrupt stays asserted and the CPU re-enters
the dispatcher forever.  On a Raspberry Pi 3B+ running rtems-libbsd
with USB Ethernet this shows up as a complete system freeze within
seconds to minutes.

Mask the value with BCM2835_IRQ_BASIC_SPEEDUP_USED_BITS before the bit
scan.  With 0x900 the USB interrupt is dispatched first; the immediate
re-entry sees 0x100, takes the IRQ_PENDING1 path and dispatches the
tick.

Tested on Raspberry Pi 3B+ (raspberrypi2 BSP, RTEMS 6.2, rtems-libbsd
6-freebsd-14, EPICS 7.0.10): more than 15 hours of continuous operation
with about 450 million USB transfers, versus a freeze within a minute
before the change.
---
 bsps/arm/raspberrypi/irq/irq.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bsps/arm/raspberrypi/irq/irq.c b/bsps/arm/raspberrypi/irq/irq.c
index ba47edf..098cf73 100644
--- a/bsps/arm/raspberrypi/irq/irq.c
+++ b/bsps/arm/raspberrypi/irq/irq.c
@@ -130,7 +130,17 @@ void bsp_interrupt_dispatch(void)
 
   pend = BCM2835_REG(BCM2835_IRQ_BASIC);
   if ( pend & BCM2835_IRQ_BASIC_SPEEDUP_USED_BITS ) {
-    pend_bit = ffs(pend) - 1;
+    /*
+     * Only the speed-up bits may be fed to ffs().  Bit 8 ("one or more
+     * bits set in pending register 1") is set as well when a non-speed-up
+     * GPU interrupt (e.g. the system timer tick, GPU IRQ 3) is pending at
+     * the same time as a speed-up one (e.g. USB, GPU IRQ 9).  ffs(pend)
+     * then selects bit 8 whose table entry is -1, no handler is called,
+     * the interrupt stays asserted and the CPU re-enters this function
+     * forever (observed as a system hang on Raspberry Pi 3B+ under USB
+     * load).
+     */
+    pend_bit = ffs(pend & BCM2835_IRQ_BASIC_SPEEDUP_USED_BITS) - 1;
     vector = bcm2835_irq_speedup_table[pend_bit];
   } else {
     pend = BCM2835_REG(BCM2835_IRQ_PENDING1);
-- 
2.43.0

